  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 258 AM MST Mon Nov 23 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview: \n\nLight winds, along with persisting snowpack, will promote areas \nof fog development tonight into Monday morning. The areas of \nhighest concern for fog development will be the northern/central \nplains and the valleys. Increasing winds, along with decreasing \nsnowpack, will limit further fog concerns as the week progresses.\n\nA period of weak upper-level ridging will begin, allowing for \nincreasing temperatures, dry conditions, and mild winds. As an upper-\nlevel trough tracks north of MT over Monday night and Tuesday, winds \nwill increase and become breezy. Stronger upper- level ridging will \ndevelop over Tuesday night, allowing for a continuation of breezy \nand dry conditions. The upper-level ridge begins to flatten over \nWednesday afternoon, resulting in westerly flow and strengthening \nwesterly winds. The main area of concern for stronger winds will be \nthe northern Rocky Mountain Front with gusts up to 45-50 mph. \nPrecipitation will be mostly blocked by the Rockies due to the \ndirection of the flow with the bulk of precip being limited to \nWestern MT. Beginning Thursday afternoon, light showers will \nbecome possible in SW MT. Precip then becomes more widespread over\nThursday night and Friday. Westerly upper-level flow will \ncontinue to strengthen through the rest of the work week, \nresulting in areas of strong westerly/northwesterly winds. Main \nareas of concern with these winds will be the eastern slopes of \nnorth-south oriented mountains. Although there is uncertainty due \nto the time until the event, peak gusts are currently expected to \nbe in the 50-70 mph range. -ESH\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nStrong Winds Thursday night through Friday\n\nGeostrophic westerly winds will favor development of strong westerly \nwinds. However, due to the amount of time until the winds are \nexpected, there is still uncertainty of whether the favorable \nconditions will actually develop. Currently, there is a >60% chance \nof winds exceeding 60 mph. -ESH",
